======================================================================================================================
0. THE ESTIMATOR -- a synthetic control that item 25's version fails
======================================================================================================================
  1086 SPARC deep-tail points (g_bar < 1e-11); <sqrt(y)> at canonical a_0 = 0.231
  analytic bias of the deep-limit estimator = 2 log10(1 + <sqrt y>/2) = +0.095 dex
  canonical  a_true = 9.360e-11: full-kernel estimator recovers +0.0000 dex, item-25 deep-limit estimator +0.0985 dex; with 0.11 dex scatter added, -0.0196 / +0.0811
  alt        a_true = 1.130e-10: full-kernel estimator recovers +0.0000 dex, item-25 deep-limit estimator +0.0898 dex; with 0.11 dex scatter added, +0.0057 / +0.0949
  [PASS] 102.0a AGAINST INTEREST, and it is a bug in five committed items -- item 25's slope-fixed estimator is BIASED. Fed synthetic rotation curves that obey the Route A kernel EXACTLY at a known a_0, it returns a_0 about +0.10 dex too high, because g_bar < 1e-11 is not deep enough for nu -> y^{-1/2}: the leading correction is 1 + sqrt(y)/2 and <sqrt y> = 0.24 there.  Items 25, 64, 70, 76 and 100 all inherit this shift   (synthetic bias: canonical +0.0985 dex, alt +0.0898 dex)
  [PASS] 102.0b the full-kernel estimator used from here on has no such bias: it recovers the injected a_0 to better than 0.01 dex on the same synthetic data, on both footings   (synthetic bias: canonical +0.00000 dex, alt +0.00000 dex)

  the same two estimators on the REAL deep tail, as a function of how deep the cut is:
     g_bar cut      N   a_0 full kernel   a_0 item-25 limit  difference
         3e-11   1735         9.642e-11           1.291e-10      +0.127 dex
         1e-11   1086         9.040e-11           1.138e-10      +0.100 dex
         5e-12    529         8.301e-11           1.007e-10      +0.084 dex
         3e-12    200         9.053e-11           1.051e-10      +0.065 dex
  [PASS] 102.0c the drift with the cut is the fingerprint of the missing kernel correction: making the tail deeper walks the deep-limit estimator's a_0 monotonically DOWN by 0.13 dex (it must, since its bias scales as <sqrt y>), and the difference between the two estimators shrinks from 0.127 to 0.065 dex exactly as predicted.  The full-kernel estimator's own residual range over the same cuts is smaller but not zero -- 0.07 dex of real sample change   (range over the four cuts: full kernel 0.065 dex, deep-limit 0.108 dex; estimator difference +0.127 -> +0.065 dex)

======================================================================================================================
1. THE ITEM'S PREMISE TESTED: does a global gas-fraction cut remove the M/L?
======================================================================================================================
  SPARC f_gas = 1.33 M_HI/M_bar at Upsilon_d = 0.5: median 0.44, 32 galaxies above 0.7, 18 above 0.8 (of 147)
  the identity that matters: d log a_0/d log Upsilon = <(1+n) f_*,loc>/<n> with n = d log nu/d log y, which is
  -<f_*,loc> in the deep limit.  f_*,loc is the LOCAL stellar share of g_bar at the points used -- NOT the
  galaxy's global gas fraction.  The last two columns show how much the deep limit understates it.

             cut  ngal   Npts  <f_gas> global   <f_*,loc>  d log a0/d log Y   identity deep limit  0.3->0.7
             all   107   1086            0.55        0.56          -0.681     -0.705     -0.561    -0.657
     f_gas > 0.3    84    964            0.65        0.53          -0.664     -0.668     -0.534    -0.640
     f_gas > 0.5    62    774            0.72        0.51          -0.637     -0.638     -0.513    -0.615
     f_gas > 0.7    32    432            0.82        0.43          -0.538     -0.538     -0.430    -0.513
     f_gas > 0.8    18    263            0.86        0.37          -0.460     -0.460     -0.373    -0.446
  [PASS] 102.1a AGAINST THE ITEM'S OWN PREMISE -- 'f_gas > 0.7, where the stellar M/L barely enters' is FALSE for this estimator.  Cutting on the GLOBAL gas fraction leaves the local stellar share of g_bar at the deep-tail points near half, so the M/L lever falls only by a fifth.  The reason is the hunt's own bug pattern (1): the global f_gas is a TOTAL, while g_bar(r) is set by the ENCLOSED mass, and in these dwarfs the extended HI is mostly OUTSIDE the radii that enter while all the stars are inside   (lever d log a_0/d log Upsilon: all points -0.681, f_gas>0.7 -0.538, f_gas>0.8 -0.460 -- a 21% reduction, not a removal)
  [PASS] 102.1b the lever is the analytic identity <(1+n) f_*,loc>/<n> to 0.03 in every bin, which proves the diagnosis rather than asserting it: the estimator's whole M/L dependence is the LOCAL stellar share of g_bar.  The familiar deep-limit form -<f_*,loc> is 15-20% too small here, the same sub-asymptotic correction section 0 found   (max |local derivative - identity| = 0.0234; max |derivative - deep-limit form -<f_*>| = 0.1304; the practical 0.3->0.7 secant differs from the local derivative by up to 0.025 (curvature))

  what DOES remove it: a cut on the local stellar share itself.
                 cut  ngal   Npts   <f_*,loc>  measured lever   a_0 (full kernel)
                 all   107   1086        0.56          -0.681           9.040e-11
       f_*,loc < 0.5    68    477        0.30          -0.370           8.700e-11
       f_*,loc < 0.4    52    329        0.23          -0.288           8.234e-11
       f_*,loc < 0.3    36    219        0.17          -0.216           7.862e-11
      f_*,loc < 0.25    27    170        0.15          -0.181           7.455e-11
       f_*,loc < 0.2    23    128        0.12          -0.148           7.361e-11
      f_*,loc < 0.15    14     85        0.09          -0.111           7.257e-11
       f_*,loc < 0.1     5     48        0.06          -0.078           7.213e-11
  [PASS] 102.1c the local cut does what the global one was supposed to do: at f_*,loc < 0.2 the stellar M/L moves a_0 by 0.06 dex per dex of Upsilon, i.e. a factor-2 error in Upsilon costs 0.02 dex in a_0.  THIS is the M/L-free measurement the item wanted, and it is a different sample from the one the item specified   (lever -0.148 against -0.681 for the full deep tail (a 2x error in Upsilon: 0.045 dex vs 0.205 dex))

