3. THE M/L-FREE MEASUREMENT (local cut), and what carries its error
======================================================================================================================
  a_0 (f_*,loc < 0.2, full kernel) = 7.361e-11 +- 0.062 dex [6.489e-11, 8.430e-11], 23 galaxies, 128 points
     vs canonical 9.360e-11: -0.104 dex (-1.7 sigma)
     vs alt       1.130e-10: -0.186 dex (-3.0 sigma)
     vs the KiDS dwarf lens stack (item 2, the other M/L-insensitive rung) 9.550e-11 +- 0.109 dex: -0.113 dex (-0.9 sigma)
  [PASS] 102.3a (the item's second criterion, and this one PASSES) the M/L-free deep tail agrees with the KiDS dwarf lens stack -- two measurements of a_0 eleven decades apart in acceleration and sharing no systematic, neither of which goes through a stellar mass-to-light ratio   (7.36e-11 vs 9.55e-11, -0.113 +- 0.126 dex)
  [PASS] 102.3b AGAINST INTEREST: with the estimator bug removed AND the M/L removed, the SPARC deep tail sits BELOW both footings, by about 1.7 sigma under canonical and 3.0 under alt.  Item 25's headline (a_0 = 1.14e-10, 'the alt footing to 0.004 dex') was the +0.10 dex estimator bias plus a stellar M/L; corrected, the alt footing is the one in trouble and the canonical is the one that survives   (corrected M/L-free a_0 = 7.361e-11; canonical -1.7 sigma, alt -3.0 sigma; item 25 quoted 1.14e-10)
  CAVEAT ON THAT SIGMA -- the errors above are the GALAXY BOOTSTRAP only.  h103_deep_tail_error_budget.py adds the
  coherent calibration terms (the distance scale above all), which take the total to 0.074 dex and make BOTH
  footings consistent.  The deep tail must not be quoted as deciding between them; what it says is that the
  central value moved down by 0.19 dex when the estimator bug and the stellar M/L were both removed.

  splits -- the M/L-free sample is small and its error is a DISTANCE error (a_0 ~ D^-2):
    distance error < 12% (TRGB/Cepheid/direct)   a_0 = 9.078e-11 +- 0.104 dex  (8 galaxies, 70 points)
    distance error >= 12% (Hubble flow)          a_0 = 5.677e-11 +- 0.077 dex  (15 galaxies, 58 points)
    inclination >= 60 deg                        a_0 = 7.176e-11 +- 0.084 dex  (12 galaxies, 89 points)
    inclination < 60 deg                         a_0 = 7.805e-11 +- 0.081 dex  (11 galaxies, 39 points)
  [PASS] 102.3c the M/L-free sample splits on DISTANCE QUALITY at 0.20 dex, 1.6 sigma: the eight galaxies with TRGB or Cepheid distances give a_0 on the canonical footing, the fifteen with 20-30% Hubble-flow distances give 40% less. Since a_0 ~ D^-2 this is where the whole error lives, and it is item 103's business   (good distances 9.078e-11 vs Hubble flow 5.677e-11: +0.204 +- 0.129 dex (+1.6 sigma))

======================================================================================================================
4. AN a_0-FREE MEASUREMENT OF UPSILON THAT FALLS OUT OF THE SAME SAMPLE
======================================================================================================================
  The deep tail contains both gas-dominated and star-dominated points.  Requiring the two to return the SAME a_0
  -- whatever a_0 is -- determines Upsilon_[3.6] with no cosmology in it at all.  (Item 76 asked the different
  question of what Upsilon reproduces Planck's a_0; this one never uses a_0.)
     Upsilon_d   a_0 (f_*,loc<0.2)     N   a_0 (f_*,loc>0.6)     N  difference
          0.30           8.594e-11   213           1.741e-10   489      +0.307 dex
          0.40           7.631e-11   162           1.221e-10   495      +0.204 dex
          0.50           7.361e-11   128           9.135e-11   472      +0.094 dex
          0.60           7.143e-11    99           7.255e-11   450      +0.007 dex
          0.70           6.955e-11    86           6.035e-11   428      -0.062 dex
          0.80           6.384e-11    72           5.172e-11   396      -0.091 dex
  the two halves of the deep tail return the same a_0 at Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.612 +- 0.150 (galaxy bootstrap, 83 successes)
  stellar populations at 3.6 um: 0.50 +- 0.10 (Schombert+2019, McGaugh+2016).  DiskMass dynamical: ~0.3.
  [PASS] 102.4 a stellar mass-to-light ratio measured WITHOUT a_0 and without stellar populations, from the internal consistency of the deep tail alone: Upsilon_[3.6] = the value that makes gas-dominated and star-dominated points return the same a_0.  It agrees with stellar populations.  AGAINST INTEREST it is NOT precise enough to decide anything: +-0.15 puts DiskMass's 0.3 only 2.0 sigma away, so 'DiskMass excluded' must NOT be quoted from this measurement -- what can be quoted is that a completely independent route lands on the SPS value   (Upsilon = 0.612 +- 0.150; vs SPS 0.50 +- 0.10 at +0.6 sigma; vs DiskMass 0.30 at only +2.0 sigma -- not an exclusion)
  AGAINST INTEREST: at that self-consistent Upsilon the deep tail's a_0 is 7.129e-11, which is -0.118 dex from canonical and -0.200 from alt.
  So the sample cannot have all three of (Upsilon = 0.5, a_0 = canonical, gas and star points agreeing).  Fixing
  the internal consistency costs ~0.1 dex in a_0; fixing a_0 costs ~0.09 dex of gas-vs-star disagreement.  That
  residual is the real M/L systematic of the deep-tail estimator and it is quoted here, not hidden.

======================================================================================================================
5. THE ALTERNATIVE COMPUTED BESIDE IT
======================================================================================================================
  Newtonian (nu = 1, same baryons, no halo): <log g_obs/g_bar> = +0.713 dex on the M/L-free points -- a factor 5 discrepancy, so these points are not Newtonian
  per-galaxy M/L-free a_0 (17 galaxies with >= 3 points): spread 0.267 dex, slope against log M_bar = -0.167 +- 0.143
  In LambdaCDM there is no a_0: the quantity measured here would be a halo property, and halo properties of dwarfs
  run with mass (V_max^4/(G M_b) tracks the stellar-to-halo mass relation, slope ~ -0.4 to -0.6 over this range).
  [PASS] 102.5 the framework's side of the alternative holds: the per-galaxy M/L-free a_0 has no significant mass trend over 1.7 decades of baryonic mass, where a halo-property reading of the same number would run with mass   (slope -0.167 +- 0.143 dex/dex (-1.2 sigma from flat); per-galaxy spread 0.267 dex, dominated by the 20-30% distances)

======================================================================================================================
6. MUTATION CONTROLS
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M102a AGAINST INTEREST, and it is a property of the estimator worth stating: permuting g_obs against g_bar changes NOTHING.  <log g_obs - log(nu(g_bar/a) g_bar)> depends only on the two marginal means, so the a_0 measured here is a NORMALISATION of the deep tail and carries no information about the RAR point by point.  A permutation mutation therefore cannot test it -- the controls that can are the injections M102b/M102c, and the shape is tested separately by the free-slope fit below and by items 22 and 25   (shuffled a_0 = 7.361152e-11, unshuffled 7.361152e-11 -- identical to machine precision)
  free log-log slope of the M/L-free points: OLS(y|x) = 0.281 +- 0.078 (galaxy bootstrap), orthogonal 0.369, inverse regression 1.218; the kernel's own local slope here is 0.553 (not 1/2 -- same sub-asymptotic correction); shuffled +0.061; correlation r = 0.480
  [PASS] M102a2 AGAINST INTEREST: the M/L-free subsample CANNOT measure the RAR slope, and its OLS value looks bad.  The cut leaves only 0.9 dex of g_bar and r = 0.48, so the forward regression is heavily attenuated: OLS gives 0.28, 3.5 sigma below the kernel's predicted 0.55 (and 2.8 below a nominal 1/2).  The errors-in-variables bracket [OLS, inverse regression] = [0.28, 1.22] does contain the prediction, so this is an absence of leverage rather than a discrepancy -- but it must be recorded as an untested assumption of the a_0 measurement, which fixes the shape and fits only the normalisation.  The shuffle control does work: permuting g_obs flattens even this weak slope to 0.06   (OLS 0.281 +- 0.078 (-3.5 sigma from the kernel's 0.553); bracket [0.28, 1.22] contains it; shuffled +0.061)
  [PASS] M102b turning the kernel off (nu = 1, g_obs = g_bar) must make the estimator run away to an absurd a_0, since no a_0 can make the boosted kernel reproduce an unboosted curve   (nu=1 synthetic returns a_0 = nan (bracket floor 1e-13))
  [PASS] M102c injecting a 4x wrong a_0 must be recovered as a 4x wrong a_0 -- the estimator's sensitivity is the textbook one and not a fixed point   (injected 3.744e-10, recovered 3.744e-10 (+0.0000 dex))

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- ITEM 102
======================================================================================================================
  The item as written does not work, for a reason worth more than the item: the global gas fraction is not what
  sets the estimator's M/L leverage.  Cutting SPARC at f_gas > 0.7 leaves the local stellar share of g_bar at
  0.43 and the M/L lever at -0.54, against -0.68 for the whole deep tail -- a 21% reduction, not a
  removal, because the HI that makes these dwarfs gas-rich lies OUTSIDE the radii where g_bar is evaluated.
  Cutting on the local stellar share instead takes the lever to -0.15, and that is the measurement:
      a_0 (M/L-free, full kernel) = 7.361e-11  +- 0.062 dex  (15%, 23 galaxies, 128 points)
      canonical -0.104 dex (-1.7 sigma) | alt -0.186 dex (-3.0 sigma) | KiDS dwarf lenses -0.113 dex (-0.9 sigma)
  It does not reach the item's 8%; it reaches 15%, and the budget is distance, not M/L (item 103).
  Two corrections go on the ledger and both cut against previously banked numbers:
    * item 25's slope-fixed estimator is biased +0.10 dex by the kernel's own sub-asymptotic
      correction.  Its a_0 = 1.14e-10 becomes 9.04e-11 on the same points with the full kernel, and the
      claim that it lands on the alt footing to 0.004 dex is withdrawn.  Items 64, 70, 76 and 100 inherit the shift.
    * the same points give an a_0-free measurement of Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.61 +- 0.15, from the requirement that
      gas-dominated and star-dominated points agree.  It sits on the SPS value and is 2.0 sigma from DiskMass's
      0.3 -- consistent, not decisive, and it must not be quoted as an exclusion.
  Nothing here is a second Kepler-grade law.  What it is, is the first rung of the ladder that item 125 needs, with
  its systematic named and an estimator bias removed from four items that were already banked.

